// The employer burden percentage — the cost of an hour BEYOND the wage and
// the CBA fringes, as the owner records it once a year.
//
// Its own file, like emr.prisma and certifications.prisma, so this lane's
// migrations do not collide with anyone else's schema edits.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. `lib/labor-cost.ts` prices a logged hour as base wage ×
// pay-type multiplier + the four CBA fringes (pension, vacation, health &
// welfare, training) and stops. There is no employer FICA in it, no
// FUTA/SUTA, no workers' compensation premium. That arithmetic is correct
// for what it claims — a certified payroll and a fringe remittance both want
// exactly that figure — but the job-cost screens presented it as the
// "burdened rate", which to a contractor means fully loaded. So
// `actualCostToDate` was understated, and cost-to-cost percent complete was
// OVERSTATED, which is the direction that hurts.
//
// WHY IT IS A RATE A PERSON TYPES IN, NOT A CALCULATION. Employer FICA is a
// published rate with a wage base per employee per calendar year; FUTA/SUTA
// is per-state, per-employer, with its own wage base and an experience
// rating; workers' comp is a manual rate per class code times the EMR times
// whatever the carrier actually wrote. This app holds none of those inputs —
// no per-employee year-to-date wages, no state unemployment account, no
// class-code rate sheet. A figure derived here would be a number the
// company's CPA has never seen. What a contractor DOES have is the
// percentage their accountant gives them, and that is what this table holds.
//
// WHAT IT IS APPLIED TO, AND THAT THIS IS A MODELLING CHOICE. The percentage
// multiplies the BASE WAGE only — not the fringes. Bona fide contributions
// to a benefit plan are generally outside FICA wages, so burdening them
// again would double-count. THAT IS A MODELLING CHOICE A CPA SHOULD CONFIRM,
// NOT A TAX RULE ANYONE HERE VERIFIED. It is stated in lib/employer-burden.ts
// and on the screen where the rate is entered, so nobody has to read a
// schema comment to find out what their number is being multiplied by.

/// One employer-burden percentage, for one company, from one date.
///
/// Company-scoped, not job-scoped and not craft-scoped: FICA, unemployment
/// and comp are employer-level costs. A company that genuinely needs a
/// per-craft burden has a different product requirement and should not get
/// it by accident from a table shaped like this one.
///
/// UNIQUE ON (companyId, effectiveDate): one rate per start date. Re-running
/// the calculation for the same date is an EDIT of that row, not a second
/// row — two rows on one date would leave "which burden applies" with no
/// honest answer.
///
/// THERE IS NO `isCurrent` COLUMN, and no "the company's burden rate" field
/// on Company. Which rate applies is DERIVED from the dates (CLAUDE.md:
/// derived state is never stored), and it is derived per TIME ENTRY, not
/// once per page: hours worked in March are costed at March's rate even
/// after April's has been recorded. A stored flag would disagree with the
/// dates the day a new rate starts, and nobody would be there to flip it.
model EmployerBurdenRate {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  companyId String
  company   Company @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])

  /// The first day this percentage applies to. ENTERED from whatever the
  /// accountant worked it out against, never stamped — `now()` would be the
  /// day somebody typed, not the day the rate took effect. UTC midnight,
  /// rendered in UTC, same as every other date in this app.
  effectiveDate DateTime

  /// The percentage itself, as a percentage and never as a fraction: 15.5
  /// means 15.5%, not 1550%. Three decimal places because a burden worked
  /// out from a payroll summary routinely lands on something like 28.375.
  /// Decimal, not Float, so 15.5 is 15.5.
  percent Decimal @db.Decimal(6, 3)

  /// What the percentage covers and where it came from — "FICA 7.65, FUTA/
  /// SUTA 2.1, comp 9.4, per Hernandez CPA 2026 review". Free text and
  /// optional: the app never parses it. It exists because a bare number on
  /// a screen a year later is a number nobody can defend to a GC.
  note String?

  createdByUserId String?
  createdBy       User?   @relation("EmployerBurdenRateCreatedBy", fields: [createdByUserId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@unique([companyId, effectiveDate])
}
